id: design.state-machine
kind: design
title: State machine
aliases: state machine, fsm, explicit states
tags: architecture, modelling
questions:
  - how do you model a job that moves through stages?
invariant: Enumerate states and legal transitions, store the current state, and make every transition idempotent.
answer_30s: Model the entity as states plus allowed transitions, persist the state, and let each transition be safe to repeat -- which is what makes retries and restarts tractable.
answer_2m: |
  The value is not diagrams, it is that illegal transitions become impossible to
  express: a job cannot be both running and completed, a payment cannot skip
  authorised. Persisting the state gives recovery for free (a crashed worker
  resumes from the stored state), gives the operator a vocabulary to query
  ("show everything stuck in awaiting_webhook"), and gives retries a defined
  meaning. Complement it with a terminal-state table and a timeout per
  non-terminal state, because the most common real bug is an entity that has
  silently stopped advancing.
when_to_use:
  - job lifecycles, payments, order fulfilment, provisioning, controllers
minimal_example: |
  pending -> running -> succeeded
      |          |
      |          +-> failed -> retrying -> running
      +-> cancelled

  transitions: explicit table; each guarded by the current state
  store: current state + updated_at (for stuck detection)
failure_modes:
  - status as a free-text column, so illegal transitions reach production
  - no timeout on non-terminal states, so stuck entities are only found by users
  - transitions with side effects that are not idempotent, so retries duplicate them
related:
  - design.idempotency
  - design.retry-backoff
  - recipe.async-job-processing
  - k8s.reconciliation
sources:
  - https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/state-watch.html
